November 28, 2010.

This is "ggg", a gglcars-based stroboscopic tuner, but redone for
Android.  Given a catchy name "TunerTime" (yay!  it's tuner time!)
because it is eventually destined for release on the Android Market,
where perhaps one of my programs will be used by more than one person.

Hah.

Anyways, I have figured out what I need to do.

android.media.AudioRecord gives me access to the microphone via read().
It is, for example, the interface used by sipdroid (open source sip
phone), so it must be pretty alright at least.  I will call its blocking
read() function over and over in a separate thread I will spawn for just
this purpose.  And that separate thread will have a Surface (or
SurfaceHolder?  I'm not sure) that it will hold onto, and that thread
will somehow copy pixels from the audio data to the Surface (through a
Parcel??).  The rest is simple UI garbage.

A real question, though, is whether it is worth keeping any JNI in this
at all.  The math involved is so simple that it's basically just a big
copy operation.  Though I think honestly that the entire thread could
live from start to finish in C, just always hanging in the JNI method
call to AudioRecord.read(), or into the Surface call to receive a parcel.

I guess I have to design a UI now.  The original has a 3-octave
stroboscopic view surrounded by buttons:
   * QUIT (unneeded on android - user will click 'home' or what-have-you)
   * PAUSE (can be just a tap of the stroboscopic display to pause/unpause, but needs PAUSE notifier!)
   * CONF -> changes other buttons to be +/- for center A (440Hz +/-1Hz
     increments), and +/- for stretch factor (5-digit decimal incremented
     by 25), and RESET (A=440, stretch=0)
   * +/- for octave, semi, and cent.
Plus a status display that provides:
   * A 3     <--- note octave
   * +0c     <--- cents
   * A=440   <--- center A frequency
   * +00005  <--- stretch factor

So I need to do a portrait mode display...

[strobe octave 1] ...
.....................
[strobe octave 2] ...
.....................
[strobe octave 3] ...
.....................
semi+ oct+  cent+ A=440
 A     3     +0c  str=00025
semi- oct-  cent- [CONF]


...

Okay, throwing together the base UI was pretty easy.  It is now fully
functional, except that it doesn't have the thread that does all the
stroboscopic stuff.  So it doesn't do anything, in other words.

Unfortunately I can't really test the stroboscope thread in the emulator.
As near as I have been able to tell from reading, it famously doesn't
have proper microphone support.  But at least the drawing primitives
ought to work, so I can test it with gibberish data.

...

I couldn't get drawing to work at all.  It seems there are supposed to be
two options for drawing from a thread.  They are both through
SurfaceView.  You can then get a SurfaceHolder from that.  From the
SurfaceHolder, you can access the Surface directly, which appears to
allow drawing using "Parcel"s somehow??  Or SurfaceHolder will return a
Canvas (lockCanvas()) object which is supposed to be valid until
unlockCanvasAndPost().  The latter seems more convenient and would allow
us to get rid of the surfaceChanged() et al hooks.  But maybe we should
keep them?  The former seems like it might be more efficient, since it's
visibly part of a performance-conscious IPC stack (so it had better not
introduce extra copying, right?).

Anyways, there is surely something mega dumb and wrong in my code,
because I can't even get it to do a line drawing.


November 29, 2010.

Yesterday I absolutely could not get SurfaceHolder to work.  Today it is
no challenge, though.  I just abandoned my Strobe thread and put together
a little SurfaceHolder thing in the main activity, and that one worked as
I expected it would immediately.  I think the problem is that before one
of the non-lockCanvas() SurfaceHolder calls probably happened in the
Strobe thread instead of the activity thread, causing it to become
corrupted from then on.  Or something.  *shrug*

...

Okay, I'm "done."  And I get this in logcat:
  W/AudioHardwareALSA( 1890): openInputStream : mInput already exists!!

It sounds kind of like the audio device is busy?  An app called
"AudioCaps" doesn't believe my device is capable of recording at all.
Other people say there are all sorts of audio bugs on Samsung devices but
I kind of think they're full of shit.

I recently installed ("Tricorder"), and it was able to read the audio
fine when it was first installed.  But now it cannot.  I suspect it left
the audio device in a bad state.

Indeed, rebooting the device made it get past that hurdle.  Now it seems
to be dying in the JNI, and/or overflowing due to being too slow to init?

Okay, it all came down to logic errors in the way I handled the buffers.
Rather pleasing to see that the moment I got something to display at all,
it was suddenly a perfectly wonderful stroboscopic tuner.  Shows that my
piano is tuned to about 428Hz, which seems plausible.  I will finally
have my tuner with me everywhere I go.  If only I could carry around my
tuning hammer!

A quick check shows that it is consuming about 50% CPU in user-space and
less than 10% in kernel space, so we are probably not CPU constrained,
though it seems like porting the spectrum analyzer over (GGH) will cause
us to really examine that figure a little more closely.


November 24, 2013.

I'd kind of like to release this thing publicly.  I need to take care of
some things.  Like documentation, an icon, and any bug fixes or features
that might be needed.

First, though, I am perplexed by the mis-implementation of stretch.
TunerTime.get_strobe_freq() determines the frequency of the lowest strobe
and honors the stretch.  But for the octave strobes, the stretch factor
is ignored (jni/strobe.c:recalculate() just does * 2.0).  This seems
wrong to me, I think especially the octave strobes should represent the
stretch, so you can tell more easily if you got the right value.

Surprisingly, ggg (the gglcars version) has the same flaw.  However, ggd
(the original fft that grew to have a strobe feature) behaves as I would
expect.  Which supports the idea, because ggd is the one that I actually
used for piano tuning...


March 1, 2014.

I have some feature requests.  One user tuning xylophones wants to be
able to view harmonics other than 1:2:4 in the 3 graphs.  He says the 6th
and 10th harmonics are more useful in tuning a xylophone.

Another user doing tempered scales wants a configuration screen to select
default cent offsets for the 12 semitones of the scale.

These are both easy requests but the UI question is daunting.  Especially
for the tempering.

The thing is that for configuring the center freq, and the stretch
factor, it really is nice to be able to see the graphs.  So I cannot
simply make a classical Android scrolling configuration activity and add
things to that.

So what I have been leaning towards is that there will be a fixed "menu"
button on the right side of the screen, and it will bring a pop-up list,
with "note", "A freq", "stretch", "temper", "harmonics", "help" options.

note: semi+/- oct+/- cent+/-
A freq: A+/- reset
stretch: stretch+/- reset
temper: semi+/- cent+/- save/enable
harmonics: harm1+/harm1- harm2+/harm2- reset
help: jump to website

How's that?  I think that's about as good as it'll get...


March 19, 2014.

It turns out, I forgot to make a landscape layout for the new panel-based
layout!  That is the cause of about a dozen crashes that users have
experienced (crash20140318).

Landscape layouts are, as usual, awkward because text tends to be wider
than it is tall.  But I think changing it from "foo+" / "foo-" buttons to
just "+" and "-" buttons, with a "foo" header over the whole shebang,
will probably suffice...

...

Okay, while rotating it a bunch to test the layout, I managed to trigger
a SIGSEGV in JNI:

   I/DEBUG   (  172):     #00  pc 000019ae  /data/app-lib/org.galexander.tunertime-1/libstrobe-jni.so (Java_org_galexander_tunertime_Strobe_runner+1797)

That was preceded by a ton of:

   E/dalvikvm(28800): JNI ERROR: env->self != thread-self (0x74cb2620 vs. 0x75899460); auto-correcting
   E/dalvikvm(28800): JNI ERROR: env->self != thread-self (0x74cb2620 vs. 0x75899460); auto-correcting
   E/dalvikvm(28800): JNI ERROR: env->self != thread-self (0x74cb2620 vs. 0x75899460); auto-correcting
   E/dalvikvm(28800): JNI ERROR: env->self != thread-self (0x74cb2620 vs. 0x75899460); auto-correcting

with a few different addresses showing up on the lhs of the "vs." No idea
what that means.

Before the JNI ERRORs, I also got this:

   W/AudioPolicyManagerBase(  177): startInput() input 486 failed: other input already started..
   E/AudioRecord(28800): start() status -38

Which might be the mysterious record problem reported by a user.  Seems
like there is a race case between shutting down the old one and starting
the new one.


May 26, 2014.

Bugs aside for the moment, I want to add the traditional tuner
functionality (detecting which note is playing, instead of being told).
I was thinking I'd give this a special mode, but really it might be
convenient to have it on all of the modes.  The UI is a bit of a
question, but I think I'm content to simply split the mode button in
half to make room for a new button.  The new button would show the
current note, and when clicked it would set the strobes to that note.

There's some debate about FFT vs. filters.  Time domain information is
not particularly meaningful, so FFT is totally viable.  However, FFT
gives me a peak-detection problem that I'm not particularly interested
in.  Anyways, I have a draft of the filter design in mind already.

So the plan is to have an 88-channel filter (like ggh), and it will
collect the output of these filters into a single variable using a
simplified very-low-pass filter, something like
    new_value = old_value*0.999 + curr_sample
Then I will sum these 88 channels into 12 channels (collapse the octaves
together), and then find the highest result, and that tells us which
semitone.  Then we search the octaves and find the highest octave, and
that's the octave.  If no channel is above a certain threshhold, it will
simply display what it was displaying last time...

...

Well, the advantages of the filter-based version were non-illusive, in
that I had no trouble implementing it.  But it is way too slow.  I
remember how I made it work on the n800 last time: I limited its input to
something like 8kHz, roughly 20% as much work as 44kHz.

Obviously, I could build for hardfp or start another thread or any number
of things to get the required performance out of my phone, but that is
ridiculous.  I forgot how expensive these filters are.  Roughly 11
floating point operations per filter (88x) per sample (44.1kHz) means
roughly 42 MFLOPS.  That's well within the realm of plausibility, but well
outside the realm of "free".

The FFT, on the other hand, seems to be roughly 10 * n * lg(n), or around
100k for 1024 samples, or closer to 4 MFLOPS.  That's better.  I'd prefer
even better yet but maybe I won't get it.  I had the idea increasing the
block size would help, but number of operations per second would be
   (44100/n)*10*n*lg(n)
   441000*lg(n)
I.e., as the block size increases, it does get more expensive per sample,
just not very quickly.  And I doubt I want to do a block size any smaller
than 1024.  In fact, 2048 is the smallest that ggd does and that looks
pretty sketchy.


July 30, 2014.

I have decided the FFT is too slow to be running "in the background" of
regular operation.  It makes the output visibly stutter.  So I intend to
separate it out into its own mode.


August 10, 2014.

So, I implemented the FFT bit, and it looks pretty good and is kind of
easy to use.  The only thing that sucks in terms of usability is that
it's easy to lose your target waveform as you zoom in.  Probably
pinch-to-zoom would be the prefered solution.

I have implemented a GUI button for hamming window, and I was going to
hold off on finishing the GUI until I knew if I really wanted that
button.  So, I've been reading about windows.

It looks like the way these things are described is by how they smear out
a perfect sinewave into neighboring bins.  The rectangular window
actually has just about the best peak at bin 0 (where the wave should be
represented, I guess), going quickly down to -20dB.  But then it has a
large range between -30dB and -40dB (-10,000x).  Other windows, such as
Hamming and so on, have a fatter peak around bin 0, but then they quickly
go down to -50dB (-100,000x) or even -100dB (-10,000,000,000x).

I think I don't care, in fact I might prefer the rectangular window for
this purpose because of the sharpness of its peak at 0.  -20dB (-100x) is
already enough that it won't hardly show up in my plot, so the
rectangular window is fine.  For other analyses, I'd care, but for this
crude visual representation the rectangular window will do just fine.

So that's my "use words to excuse yourself from implementing a Hamming
window."


September 13, 2014.

Trying to track down the bug that causes it to lose microphone input.
I'm heartened that the bug is present on Nexus 7 (changing orientation
seems to be enough), though I haven't had a chance to test on there since
I added debugging messages...

I did get a SIGSEGV when changing orientation on my Moto G
(crash20140913-1).  It is taking a bad branch to 0.  The blx call is at
.text+2b3e in JNI.  it seems to be
   *(*((3a800000+pc)+16)+223*4)
Not sure what that means.  Let's try to find source correspondence....

Okay, I got it to crash with the debugging messages, and it looks like
what is happening is that the previous thread never dies!  I get a thread
starting up.  startRecording() fails:

   I/System.out(20796): tt: startRecording
   W/AudioPolicyManagerBase( 274): startInput() input 16759 failed: other
   input already started..
   E/AudioRecord(20796): start() status -38

But it doesn't throw an exception, and it has no return value, so my
program doesn't know until it goes to read, which then returns -3 (ESRCH
No such process?).  Meanwhile the thread that never died continues
running, but now it is generating a bunch of:

   E/dalvikvm(20796): JNI ERROR: env->self != thread-self (0x5f00c570 vs.
   0x5efd9a10); auto-correcting

That means that the env pointer comes from the wrong thread, which
happens because the env is a static variable in the JNI, so when the new
thread starts up, it replaces the old thread's env pointer.

So we really need some thread discipline to make sure that (a) the old
thread stops, and (b) the new thread doesn't start unless the old thread
stops.

I would guess that there is somewhere where interrupted can get cleared
without throwing anything or returning a useful error.  I only call
isInterrupted(), which shouldn't clear the flag.

Another possibility is that the new thread is being started before the
old thread can register its interruptedness...then the old thread never
sees it is interrupted because the env pointer was overwritten?  Seems
like a stretch because it really should depend on the "this" pointer
instead of env, but threads have a way of being magical, and I have no
idea what "auto-correcting" really means.


September 14, 2014.

I decided it's all got to be thread problems, since the JNI uses static
variables and the Java doesn't.  So I added a static variable for the
current running Strobe thread, and a Semaphore to ensure only one thread
runs at a time.  So far so good.  Knock on wood.  I am closing this crash
issue.


November 29, 2014.

Klaus Knupfer sent a list of feature requests...  I'm putting a bunch of
them at the end.


April 17, 2016.

This has not been touched in a while, and in fact I don't use it too
often.  Mostly I use the FFT mode as a novelty to visualize bird and
insect song.

However, I recently got GGI to manage the FFT->IFFT correctly, and from
that I believe that TunerTime FFT would benefit from windowing, which
will reduce the amount of a tone which is smeared across the high
frequencies.  Also, I think I will switch to GGI's technique of
representing intensity with a rainbow, which provides a much larger range
of perceptible gradations.

I'm tempted to switch to FFTW (optimized FFT library) but I don't know of
any performance problems with the existing situation so best to avoid the
effort.

Also, some of the suggestions from Klaus are worth implementing, though
working with ggi has particularly convinced me there is little advantage
to making TunerTime act more like an off-line viewer.  I think designing
it like limited bench equipment was a sound decision.



